We need a command that checks unique lines of text. That command is uniq
From the manual
    NAME
           uniq - Report or omit repeated lines.
    -c, --count
           prefix lines by the number of occurrences
    -u, --unique
           only print unique lines

    bandit8@bandit:~$ uniq -c data.txt 
          1 9E3UGT9z02VsqTV4nvnLWD5pOCYpeMpu
          1 O5ffmD1gTxN6wqrVj7s4qCGjViq2Eqa6
          1 I8fJN0v9oiPS7HFj6zQJK32Jyn7mYw2Y
          1 U6fRtWGxxx2JgitTcRpI8Eni5yrBX5uN
          1 vUvJ0fcRGiyPVnxkyQvYT0EPmlYIsUMQ
If we just use the uniq command with the -c option, it doesn't seem to work. This is due to the file not being sorted.
The file must be sorted for the uniq command to properly work as seen in the manual.
    
    EXTRA
           Filter adjacent matching lines from INPUT (or standard input), writing to OUTPUT (or standard output).
    
           Note: uniq does not detect repeated lines unless they are adjacent.  You may want to sort the input first, or use sort -u without uniq.
